			]
		},
		{
			"id": "tS3ksVcjOo0vzxFJ3",
			"name": "Will",
			"local_notes": "Lore: An Inquisitor's will has to outlast interrogation, daemonic influence, and their own doubts.\n\nDesign: Will 17 (+2 above the IQ-derived default of 15), 5 pts/level (B16).",
			"tags": [
				"Attribute"
			],
			"base_points": 10,
			"features": [
				{
					"type": "attribute_bonus",
					"attribute": "will",
					"amount": 2
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "txM7GK5eCW4eQVdrI",
			"name": "Fit",
			"reference": "B55",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Kept sharp by a career that regularly turns violent.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Fit (B55), 5 points.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": 5
		},
		{
			"id": "tdAvzztkuxiyal_Hu",
			"name": "Combat Reflexes",
			"reference": "B43",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A survivor of more ambushes and firefights than almost anyone outside the Astartes.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Combat Reflexes (B43), 15 points.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": 15
		},
		{
			"id": "tYsNbKKyXs6xq4Y6R",
			"name": "Legal Enforcement Powers (Inquisitorial Rosette)",
			"reference": "B65",
			"local_notes": "Lore: \"I have the authority of the Emperor Himself\" — an Inquisitor can commandeer Imperial Guard regiments, override a planetary governor's orders, or execute an Astartes Chapter Master's aide without trial, per repeated scenes across Eisenhorn, Ravenor, and countless codex vignettes.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Legal Enforcement Powers (B65) at its maximum, 4 levels (20 points): the right to arrest, search, requisition and execute. LEP covers what an Inquisitor may do to people; their authority over other Imperial institutions (commandeering a regiment, overruling a governor) is Inquisitorial Rank, and their standing in Imperial society is Status. Take all three.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium",
				"Inquisition"
			],
			"points_per_level": 5,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 4
		},
		{
			"id": "trGkM6641Zn66hkEO",
			"name": "Inquisitorial Rank",
			"reference": "B29",
			"local_notes": "Levels: 1 Acolyte in formal service, 3 Interrogator, 6 Inquisitor, 7 senior/renowned Inquisitor, 8 Lord Inquisitor (Grand Master of an Ordo or Inquisitorial Representative to the High Lords sit at the same ceiling; Basic Set Rank stops at 8, B29).\n\nLore: The Inquisition answers to no Imperial body but itself. A single Inquisitor can requisition Guard regiments and Navy vessels, declare Exterminatus, and relieve a governor, as shown repeatedly in the Eisenhorn and Ravenor novels and codex background. In practice that authority is limited by what the Inquisitor can enforce and by rival Inquisitors.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Rank (B29, capped at 8 levels), 5/level, with a custom +100% enhancement because the Rank reaches across every Imperial institution instead of one. Inquisitor at level 6: 30 x 2 = 60 points. Kept separate from Status per GURPS convention: Rank is authority within (here, over) organisations; Status is social standing.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium",
				"Inquisition"
			],
			"modifiers": [
				{
					"id": "mYp7sIPFK2529TgNJ",
					"name": "Overrides all Imperial hierarchies",
					"local_notes": "Every Imperial organisation (Astra Militarum, Administratum, Ecclesiarchy, Navy, planetary governments) treats this as a Rank higher than any of its own at the same or lower level. The Adeptus Astartes and Mechanicus are sovereign allies rather than subordinates: they are not bound by it, but in practice rarely defy it openly.\n",
					"cost_adj": "+100%"
				}
			],
			"points_per_level": 5,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 6,
			"max_levels": 8
		},
		{
			"id": "tHrHWMEoOFAk4_jpm",
			"name": "Status (Inquisitor)",
			"reference": "B28",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Nobles and planetary rulers defer to an Inquisitor who reveals themselves; most Imperial citizens will never meet anyone higher. Many Inquisitors hide their Status behind a cover identity until it is needed.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Status (B28) level 5 (25 points): just below a planetary governor (Status 6) socially, while Inquisitorial Rank lets them overrule one. Interrogators typically have Status 2-3.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium",
				"Inquisition"
			],
			"base_points": 25
		},
		{
			"id": "tCLK5yO804HUNHBMV",
			"name": "Sense of Duty (The Imperium)",
			"reference": "B153",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Unlike an acolyte's enforced Duty, an Inquisitor answers mostly to their own conscience and their Ordo's mandate — but that mandate (\"protect humanity, whatever it costs\") is still a real, large-scope obligation, per how both Eisenhorn and Ravenor are written as unable to simply walk away from a case.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Sense of Duty (B153), very large scope (humanity/the Imperium), -10.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": -10
		},
		{
			"id": "tzjzHe39XiqUuLEbu",
			"name": "Fanaticism (The God-Emperor)",
			"reference": "B136",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The default assumption for a loyalist Inquisitor. A Radical build that has drifted from orthodox faith (rare, but present in the fiction) may drop this with GM agreement — see the Puritan/Radical note above.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Fanaticism (B136), flat -15, taken for \"the Emperor/the Imperial Creed\" as the group/ideal. Not leveled in Basic Set. The Guardsman template carries it disabled: enable it for zealot regiments or characters, and conscript, penal and feral troops leave it off.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": -15
		},
		{
			"id": "t_I38wNQ6VXl54ou6",
			"name": "Intolerance (Heretics)",
			"reference": "B140",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Even a Radical Inquisitor is still, definitionally, opposed to Chaos.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Intolerance (B140), narrow-category cost (-5).",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": -5
		},
		{
			"id": "tDk5V1sweD5IWB_hn",
			"name": "Callous",
			"reference": "B125",
			"local_notes": "Lore: \"The Emperor Protects\" is frequently shown, in Eisenhorn especially, to mean acolytes, bystanders, and whole populations are expendable against the mission.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Callous (B125), -5.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": -5
		},
		{
			"id": "tooBD48tJ2rsmOaMN",
			"name": "Trusts No One Completely, Including Their Own Retinue",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A recurring Eisenhorn/Ravenor beat — even loyal acolytes get investigated eventually.\n\nDesign: Quirk (B9), roleplaying flag only.",
			"tags": [
				"Quirk",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": -1
		},
		{
			"id": "txAwzzviqR6ycEYkH",
			"name": "Carries the Weight of Every Call Made Alone",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Inquisitorial authority is effectively unchecked in the field, which both novels treat as a burden as much as a privilege.\n\nDesign: Quirk (B9), roleplaying flag only.",
			"tags": [
				"Quirk",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": -1
		},
		{
